      "title": "A Letter About the Library",
      "text": "Dear Editor,\n\nWhen the council suggested closing our library on Saturdays, our first request for it to stay open was met with (7) ____ . Several residents doubted that another letter would achieve anything. The librarian urged us not to jump to (8) ____ before checking the facts.\n\nWe studied the visitor records. Borrowing had fallen, but attendance at children's activities had risen. One total could not tell the whole story. It would be difficult to (9) ____ a convincing case without accurate figures showing these different uses.\n\nOur first draft asked only for more books. That would (10) ____ the point: the library also needed a safe, comfortable building. Its leaking roof was an (11) ____ problem. Faded paint could wait, but water dripping near books and electrical equipment could not.\n\nWe divided the work, each volunteer (12) ____ responsibility for one part. Some collected repair estimates while others asked families which services they used. A builder explained why patching the same leak repeatedly might cost more than repairing it properly.\n\nWe wanted the council to take our proposal (13) ____ , so we checked every figure and explained where it came from. We also included suggestions for sharing costs rather than assuming the council could pay for everything.\n\nThe council has now agreed to discuss our proposal. Approval is not (14) ____ : no decision has been made. Still, we have given the councillors a reason to look again, and that is a more useful beginning than simply saying we are disappointed.\n\nYours sincerely,\nA library volunteer"
