    "poem": {
      "title": "The Garden at Dusk",
      "text": "By day I hurry down the path,\npast leaning pots and folding chairs;\nI see the gate, the empty tray,\nthe row of steps that need repairs.\n\nAt dusk I sit upon the step.\nThe garden loosens, stitch by stitch:\na beetle lifts a fallen leaf,\na frog calls once beyond the ditch.\n\nA snail writes slowly on a stone,\nits silver sentence not yet done.\nA moth slips past the window glass,\nthen fades where darker shadows run.\n\nThe gate still leans. The tray is dry.\nNo hidden door has opened wide.\nYet paths I never saw by day\nare busy on the other side.\n\nI reach to wake the porch's lamp,\nthen leave my restless fingers still.\nThe frog begins its note again;\na cricket answers by the sill.\n\nI carry nothing through the gate,\nno prize to put upon a shelf;\nyet leave with more than I brought in:\na world still busy by itself."
    },
    "gaps": {
      "title": "Borrow, Ride, Return",
      "text": "Last spring, a group in the fictional town of Bellford collected bicycles that their owners no longer used. Volunteers repaired them and planned to lend them from a room beside the library. Photographs of the neat rows looked promising, but the photographs did not explain how a lending service would work. [20] Who could borrow a bicycle, how long could they keep it, and what should happen if it came back late?\n\nOne question concerned payment. Some volunteers wanted to charge for every ride; others wanted the service to be free. They eventually settled on a small refundable deposit. [21] If the bicycle came back on time and undamaged, the borrower received the money back.\n\nOn opening day, several families arrived together. There were plenty of bicycles available, so nobody expected a problem. [22] Without smaller frames to offer the children, the volunteers could not lend every family the bicycles it needed. Their collection had been generous, but it did not yet match the people who wanted to use it.\n\nAs suitable bicycles were added, another difficulty appeared. Every returned bicycle needed an inspection before being lent again. On busy mornings, volunteers sometimes forgot which ones had already been checked. [23] From then on, a green tag meant ready to ride, while a red tag meant that an inspection was still needed.\n\nDuring the second month, the number of loans fell. Before concluding that people had lost interest, the volunteers compared the weather records for both months. The first month had been mostly dry; the second included four wet Saturdays. [24] They decided to collect more information before changing the service.\n\nSix months later, the volunteers still counted loans, but also listened to borrowers. One person used a bicycle while saving for her own. Another borrowed one only when a relative visited. Neither rode every week, yet both valued the service. [25] Rather than judging the project only by how quickly the rows emptied, the group began asking whether the right bicycles were available when people needed them.",
      "instructions": "Use each sentence A–G once. One is not needed."
    },
    "extracts": {
      "title": "What a Map Can Tell Us",
      "text": "Extract A · A route for everyone\n\nI offered to draw the route from the station to our school fair because I knew a quick way through the park. My first map included a steep flight of steps. When my aunt said she would bring my cousin in a pushchair, I realised that the quickest route for me was not necessarily the most useful one for her. I drew a second route, longer but without steps. Before printing it, I walked it with the pushchair: a path that looked wide enough on paper narrowed beside a hedge. We found a better entrance and marked it clearly. At the fair, another visitor thanked me for showing the benches along the way. I had included them as places for tired adults to stop; his small children needed the rests just as much.\n\nExtract B · A useful kind of incompleteness\n\nA map is not a smaller copy of everything in a place. If every tree, drain and fence appeared on a street map, the roads might become difficult to find. The maker chooses details according to the map's job. A walking map and a map of underground pipes can describe the same neighbourhood but include quite different things. The maker must also choose symbols that remain readable. A narrow path may be drawn with a thick line so that you can follow it easily; that line does not mean the real path is wide. A blank patch on the paper need not be an empty patch of land. Reading a map well involves asking both what its marks tell you and what its maker has chosen not to show.\n\nExtract C · The town in the drawer\n\nGrandad's old map was coming apart along its folds. I offered to replace it with a new one, but he said that the new version would not show his town. At first I thought he meant the streets: most of them were still there. Then he pointed to the corner where he had waited for his first bus to work, and to a shop whose owner had saved broken boxes for his family. The shop had gone before I was born. To me, that corner had always been a car park. I could follow his finger across the paper, but I could only imagine the place he remembered. We put clear tape along the torn folds. Afterwards, I wrote his stories on a separate sheet and slipped it into the drawer with the map.\n\nExtract D · A map that would not stay still\n\nFor our class project, I mapped the sounds around the playground. I marked birds beside a tree and traffic near the gate. Then I returned at lunchtime: voices now covered sounds I had heard clearly before school. One drawing could not represent both visits, so I made a separate map for each time. At first I also thought the tree caused the quiet patch nearby. My teacher asked what else had changed. I noticed that the patch was farther from the road as well. My maps showed where different sounds had been heard, but they could not, by themselves, tell me what had caused every difference. On our display I wrote down the times of the visits, along with the things I still did not know.",
      "instructions": "Choose the extract that best fits. An extract may be used more than once."
    }
